# Tomcat's HTTP/2 HPACK decoder accepted any bytes a client sent in a header
# field name or value. HpackDecoder.readHpackString decoded the length-prefixed
# string literal straight off the connection's ByteBuffer and
# HPackHuffman.decode walked the Huffman tree appending each decoded
# character, and neither path asked whether the character is legal in an HTTP
# field: names are not checked against the token grammar or rejected for
# upper case, values are not checked against field-vchar/field-content, so
# control characters and forbidden bytes reached the header store that every
# downstream consumer (logging, proxying, the servlet API) trusts. The HTTP/1.1
# parser had enforced exactly this via HttpParser for years; the HTTP/2 path
# never validated at all, so over HTTP/2 the container accepted headers it
# would have rejected over HTTP/1.1 (CWE-20, CWE-113 adjacent).
#
# The fixed revision threads an isFieldName flag through both decode paths and validates
# every character before it is appended:
#
#     char c = (char) (buffer.get() & 0xFF);
#     if (isFieldName) {
#         if (HttpParser.isToken(c) && !Character.isUpperCase(c)) {
#             stringBuilder.append(c);
#         } else {
#             throw new IllegalArgumentException(...);
#         }
#     } else {
#         if ((i == 0 || i == length - 1) && HttpParser.isFieldVChar(c) ||
#                 i > 0 && i < length - 1 && HttpParser.isFieldContent(c)) {
#             stringBuilder.append(c);
#         } else {
#             throw new IllegalArgumentException(...);
#         }
#     }
#
# Pinned as a miss: nothing reports either revision. Both snippets below are
# the real decode pair (vulnerable first, patched second) and the scanner
# holds no finding on them -- measured over three --cache off runs of the
# whole java/org/apache/coyote/http2 subtree per revision: 3 findings each
# time, identical fingerprints, all VYQL-INJ-009 log findings in
# Http2UpgradeHandler, nothing on HpackDecoder or HPackHuffman. `vyql match`
# on the vulnerable HpackDecoder labels nothing at all -- source, sink and
# control all empty -- and on HPackHuffman only an integer-shift fact.
#
# No binding is shippable for the shape, for reasons the corpus measures:
#
# - The defect is a property of the whole decode-and-store path, and neither
#   end carries a label. A source binding for the decode would have to name
#   readHpackString / HPackHuffman.decode -- package-private Tomcat internals
#   with no library documentation, so the genericity test fails -- or label
#   raw ByteBuffer reads, which are ordinary NIO on every Java program.
# - The parser-property route (the AmbiguousMessageBodyFraming style:
#   identify the method by what it does, negate the remedies) has no precise
#   positive trigger. The only generic one -- a loop appending chars read
#   from a buffer into a builder -- occurs at 194 sites in 68 Java files
#   across the sibling corpus clones alone (thrift's TJSONProtocol, JSch's
#   KnownHosts and ProxyHTTP, Tika's RTF and PSD parsers, Tomcat's own
#   URLEncoder, StandardSessionIdGenerator and AbstractAccessLogValve) and at
#   46 sites inside Tomcat, including the *safe* validating HTTP/1.1 form,
#   HttpParser.readToken's `while (c != -1 && isToken(c)) result.append((char)
#   c);` -- the same textual append, guarded only by its position in the loop
#   condition, a fact no frontend emits. Validation also legitimately lives
#   in the caller or the header store in other designs, where a
#   function-scoped negation cannot see it (the measured attribution-gap
#   family).
#
# VYQL-INJ-247 (netty's documented validateHeaders switch turned off) is the
# adjacent shipped rule and is rejected on both snippets deliberately: this
# code disables no flag, and widening that concept to "a decoder with no
# validation anywhere" is the unshippable shape above, not coverage.

test "hpack header field decoded from request bytes without character validation"
  lang java
  reject VYQL-INJ-247
  code
  ```
  import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

  class HpackDecoder {
      // Decodes a HPACK string literal -- an HTTP/2 header field name or
      // value -- from the request bytes.
      private String readHpackString(ByteBuffer buffer) {
          byte data = buffer.get(buffer.position());
          int length = Hpack.decodeInteger(buffer, 7);
          boolean huffman = (data & 0b10000000) != 0;
          if (huffman) {
              return readHuffmanString(length, buffer);
          }
          StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder(length);
          for (int i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
              stringBuilder.append((char) buffer.get());
          }
          return stringBuilder.toString();
      }

      private String readHuffmanString(int length, ByteBuffer buffer) {
          StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder(length);
          HPackHuffman.decode(buffer, length, stringBuilder);
          return stringBuilder.toString();
      }
  }
  ```

test "hpack header field decode validating every character"
  lang java
  reject VYQL-INJ-247
  code
  ```
  import java.nio.ByteBuffer;

  class HpackDecoder {
      private String readHpackString(ByteBuffer buffer, boolean isFieldName) {
          byte data = buffer.get(buffer.position());
          int length = Hpack.decodeInteger(buffer, 7);
          boolean huffman = (data & 0b10000000) != 0;
          if (huffman) {
              return readHuffmanString(length, buffer, isFieldName);
          }
          StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder(length);
          for (int i = 0; i < length; ++i) {
              char c = (char) (buffer.get() & 0xFF);
              if (isFieldName) {
                  if (HttpParser.isToken(c) && !Character.isUpperCase(c)) {
                      stringBuilder.append(c);
                  } else {
                      throw new IllegalArgumentException("illegal character in name");
                  }
              } else {
                  if ((i == 0 || i == length - 1) && HttpParser.isFieldVChar(c) ||
                          i > 0 && i < length - 1 && HttpParser.isFieldContent(c)) {
                      stringBuilder.append(c);
                  } else {
                      throw new IllegalArgumentException("illegal character in value");
                  }
              }
          }
          return stringBuilder.toString();
      }

      private String readHuffmanString(int length, ByteBuffer buffer, boolean isFieldName) {
          StringBuilder stringBuilder = new StringBuilder(length);
          HPackHuffman.decode(buffer, length, stringBuilder, isFieldName);
          return stringBuilder.toString();
      }
  }
  ```
